course: dictionary
lesson: 11039
title: One word, many meanings: wear
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Wear is a good example. «Teeth are brushed gently in small circles with a soft brush, because hard scrubbing hurts the gums and wears the teeth.» Here wears means slowly damages, not clothes.
explain: «UN peacekeepers wear blue helmets and help keep a ceasefire without taking sides.» Here wear means to have something on your body. Ask yourself: is this about clothes or things on the body, or about damage from use?
explain: Wear can also be a noun, a thing you can name. «Sportswear» means clothes of one kind, for sport. Look for clues in the words around it: a word like sports, or words like scrubbing and hurts, show you which meaning fits.
fact: Wear means to have clothes, shoes, glasses or jewellery on your body.
fact: Wear can also mean to become thin, old or broken from use, as in «wear out» and «wear away».
fact: «Worn out» can mean very tired, or old and broken from use.
fact: As a noun, wear means clothes of one kind, like «sportswear», or damage from long use.
fact: A good check is to swap in «have on» for wear: if it still makes sense, wear means to have something on your body.
exercise: «Small details like a car's colour, a number plate or what someone wore help police a lot.» In this sentence, what does wore mean? | Became old and broken | Washed away by rain | Shouted loudly | *Had on, as clothes
exercise: «Teeth are brushed gently in small circles, because hard scrubbing wears the teeth.» What does wears mean here? | Hurts the tongue | Makes them whiter | Brushes the teeth clean | *Slowly damages them over time
exercise: Ella says, «My trainers are worn out.» What does worn out most likely mean? | Too big to fit | Dyed a new colour | Brand new and shiny | *Old, thin or broken from use
test: Maya says, «I am worn out after the match.» What does worn out mean? | Happy and full of energy | *Very tired | Covered in mud | Wearing a new kit
test: «Rain and wind slowly wear away the rocks over many years.» What does wear away mean here? | To paint a rock | To jump over a rock | *To slowly make something smaller or thinner | To find a new rock
exam: What does wear mean in the word «sportswear»? | Rain on a playing field | Damage from long use | *Clothes of one kind, for sport | A sport played in winter
